{"packet_id":"D025-02","doc_id":"D025","jurisdiction":"CA","category":"security_deposits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Prohibition on 'nonrefundable' security clauses","requirement":"A lease or rental agreement may not contain a provision characterizing any security as 'nonrefundable.'","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"Leases or rental agreements for residential tenancies under Cal. Civ. Code § 1950.5.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":null},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"Cal. Civ. Code § 1950.5","quoted_span":"(n) A lease or rental agreement shall not contain a provision characterizing any security as “nonrefundable.”","interaction":null,"confidence":0.95,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D025-02","doc_id":"D025","jurisdiction":"CA","category":"security_deposits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Bad faith security deposit retention damages","requirement":"A landlord who in bad faith claims or retains a security deposit, or bad faith demands replacement security, may owe statutory damages of up to twice the security in addition to actual damages, and bears the burden of proof.","key_value":"up to 2x the security deposit","coverage_conditions":"Landlords and their successors in interest holding security under Cal. Civ. Code § 1950.5.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":null},"exemptions":null,"penalty":"Statutory damages of up to twice the amount of the security in addition to actual damages; landlord bears the burden of proof on reasonableness of amounts claimed or authority to demand additional deposits.","effective_date":null,"citation":"Cal. Civ. Code § 1950.5","quoted_span":"(m) The bad faith claim or retention by a landlord or the landlord’s successors in interest of the security or any portion thereof in violation of this section, or the bad faith demand of replacement of security in violation of subdivision (k), may subject the landlord or the landlord’s successors in interest to statutory damages of up to twice the amount of the security, in addition to actual damages.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.95,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
